= 1E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= Solution
{parent=1E}

A <quadratic form> on a finite-dimensional real <vector space> $V$ is a function $q(v)=B(v,v)$ for some symmetric <bilinear form> $B$. A <positive-definite quadratic form> satisfies $q(v)>0$ for every $v\ne0$.

Completing squares gives
$$
x^2+2xy+2y^2+2yz+3z^2=(x+y)^2+(y+z)^2+2z^2.
$$
Set $u=x+y$, $v=y+z$, $w=z$. The corresponding ordered basis in the original coordinates is
$$
\boxed{(1,0,0),\ (-1,1,0),\ (1,-1,1),}
$$
and the diagonal form is $u^2+v^2+2w^2$. All diagonal coefficients are positive, so **the form is positive definite**.

= 2G
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Groups, Rings and Modules}

= a
{parent=2g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the presentation $D_6=\langle r,s:r^3=s^2=1, srs=r^{-1}\rangle$. Its subgroup $\langle r\rangle$ is the unique subgroup of order $3$, hence is a <characteristic subgroup>. An <automorphism> must send $r$ to $r$ or $r^{-1}$ and $s$ to one of the three reflections $r^js$. Thus there are at most six automorphisms.

The <center of a group> of $D_6$ is trivial, so conjugation embeds $D_6$ as a group of six <inner automorphisms>. These exhaust the possibilities. Hence **every automorphism of $D_6$ is inner**.

= b
{parent=2g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $D_8=\langle r,s:r^4=s^2=1, srs=r^{-1}\rangle$, the symmetry group of a square. The map
$$
r\mapsto r,\qquad s\mapsto rs
$$
preserves the relations and is an automorphism. Conjugation by a rotation sends $s$ to $r^{2j}s$, while conjugation by a reflection also inverts $r$. Therefore no conjugation fixes $r$ and sends $s$ to $rs$. This is an **outer automorphism of the nonabelian group $D_8$**.

= 3F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis II}

= Solution
{parent=3F}

The <Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem> in $\mathbb R$ says that every bounded real sequence has a convergent subsequence. For a bounded sequence in $\mathbb R^n$, successively extract subsequences on which the first, second, and so on through the $n$th coordinate converge. The final diagonal subsequence converges in <Euclidean space>, proving the theorem in $\mathbb R^n$.

Put $K=D\setminus\bigcup_{z\in S}B_\varepsilon(z)$. Suppose the asserted $\delta$ did not exist. Then there would be $x_k\in D$ and $y_k\in K$ with $\|x_k-y_k\|<1/k$ but $|f(x_k)-f(y_k)|\geq\varepsilon$. Since $K$ is closed and bounded, Bolzano-Weierstrass gives a subsequence $y_k\to y\in K$, and then $x_k\to y$. The point $y$ is not in $S$, because otherwise $y\in B_\varepsilon(y)$, so $f$ has <continuity> at $y$. Both $f(x_k)$ and $f(y_k)$ tend to $f(y)$, a contradiction. Hence the required **uniform $\delta>0$ exists**.

= 4F
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Complex Analysis}

= a
{parent=4f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Cauchy integral formula> and its differentiated form are
$$
f(a)=\frac1{2\pi i}\oint_C\frac{f(z)}{z-a}\,dz,
\qquad
f'(a)=\frac1{2\pi i}\oint_C\frac{f(z)}{(z-a)^2}\,dz.
$$
If $f$ is entire and $|f|\leq M$, the <Cauchy estimate> gives $|f'(a)|\leq M/\rho$. Letting $\rho\to\infty$ yields $f'(a)=0$ for every $a$. Thus **$f$ is constant**, which is <Liouville theorem>.

= b
{parent=4f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The inequality implies $u(u-1)\geq v^2\geq0$, so $u\leq0$ or $u\geq1$. Since $u$ is continuous and $\mathbb C$ is <connected>, its image lies wholly in one of these intervals. If $u\geq1$, then $g$ has no zero and $|1/g|\leq1$; <Liouville theorem> makes $1/g$, hence $g$, constant. If $u\leq0$, then $|g-1|\geq1$, so the same argument applied to $1/(g-1)$ proves that **$g$ is constant**.

= 5A
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Methods}

= Solution
{parent=5A}

<Separation of variables> with $u=X(x)Y(y)$ and the three homogeneous boundary conditions gives $X_n=\sin(n\pi x)$ and $Y_n=\sinh(n\pi y)$. The boundary value at $y=1$ contains only the third <Fourier sine series> mode. Therefore
$$
\boxed{u(x,y)=2\frac{\sinh(3\pi y)}{\sinh(3\pi)}\sin(3\pi x).}
$$
This <harmonic function> has all four prescribed boundary values.

= 6B
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=6B}

The <Born rule> and <probability current> in one dimension give
$$
\rho=|\Psi|^2,
\qquad
j=\frac{\hbar}{2mi}\left(\Psi^*\Psi_x-\Psi\Psi_x^*\right).
$$
Multiply the <time-dependent Schrodinger equation> by $\Psi^*$, subtract its complex conjugate multiplied by $\Psi$, and rearrange to obtain the <continuity equation> $\rho_t+j_x=0$.

For the stated superposition, the interference terms in $j$ are real and cancel from its imaginary part. Hence
$$
\boxed{j=\frac{\hbar k}{m}\left(|A|^2-|B|^2\right),}
$$
independent of $x$ and $t$.

= 7C
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Electromagnetism}

= Solution
{parent=7C}

Taking the <divergence> of the <Ampère-Maxwell equation> and using the <Gauss's law> $\nabla\cdot E=\rho/\varepsilon_0$ gives
$$
0=\mu_0\nabla\cdot J+\mu_0\frac{\partial\rho}{\partial t},
$$
which is <charge conservation from Maxwell equations> $\rho_t+\nabla\cdot J=0$.

By <Ohm's law>, $J=\sigma E$, so $\nabla\cdot J=\sigma\rho/\varepsilon_0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\rho(t)=\rho(0)e^{-\sigma t/\varepsilon_0},}
$$
with charge-relaxation rate $\sigma/\varepsilon_0$ and time $\varepsilon_0/\sigma$.

= 8D
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=8D}

<Gaussian elimination> without pivoting gives
$$
A=LU,
\quad
L=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0&0\\2&1&0&0\\1&3&1&0\\2&2&2&1\end{pmatrix},
\quad
U=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&1&2\\0&1&3&2\\0&0&3&6\\0&0&0&\lambda-20\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Thus $\det A=3(\lambda-20)$ and the solution is unique exactly when **$\lambda\ne20$**.

For $\lambda=20$, <forward substitution in a triangular system> in $Ly=b$ gives $y=(1,1,3,\mu-10)^T$. Consistency of $Ux=y$ requires $\boxed{\mu=10}$. <backward substitution in a triangular system> then gives
$$
\boxed{x=(4-8t,\ 4t-2,\ 1-2t,\ t)^T,\qquad t\in\mathbb R.}
$$

= 9H
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Markov Chains}

= a
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A distribution $\pi$ is an <invariant distribution> when
$$
\pi_j=\sum_{i\in S}\pi_i p_{ij}
$$
for every $j$, equivalently $\pi P=\pi$.

= b
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Detailed balance> means $\pi_i p_{ij}=\pi_jp_{ji}$ for all $i,j$. Summing over $i$ gives
$$
\sum_i\pi_i p_{ij}=\pi_j\sum_i p_{ji}=\pi_j,
$$
so **detailed balance implies invariance**.

= c
{parent=9h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $D_i$ be the <degree of a vertex> and put
$$
\pi_i=\frac{D_i}{\sum_kD_k}.
$$
If $i$ and $j$ are adjacent, then $\pi_ip_{ij}=1/\sum_kD_k=\pi_jp_{ji}$; if they are not adjacent, both sides vanish. Thus the walk is in detailed balance, and its invariant distribution is **proportional to vertex degree**.

= 10E
{parent=Paper 4}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= Solution
{parent=10E}

A <self-adjoint operator> $T$ satisfies $\langle Tx,y\rangle=\langle x,Ty\rangle$. If $Tx=\lambda x$, then $\lambda\langle x,x\rangle=\langle Tx,x\rangle=\langle x,Tx\rangle=\overline\lambda\langle x,x\rangle$, so $\lambda\in\mathbb R$. An eigenvector exists over $\mathbb C$; its <orthogonal complement> is $T$-invariant. Induction on dimension produces an <orthonormal basis> of eigenvectors, proving the <finite-dimensional spectral theorem>.

A self-adjoint $T$ is positive definite exactly when every eigenvalue is positive, since $\langle Tx,x\rangle=\sum_i\lambda_i|x_i|^2$ in an eigenbasis. The quadratic forms of two positive-definite operators add, so their sum is positive definite.

The same eigenbasis shows that $\operatorname{spec}T\subset[a,b]$ exactly when $T-\lambda I$ is positive definite for every $\lambda<a$ and negative definite for every $\lambda>b$. Finally, if $v$ is a unit eigenvector of $\alpha+\beta$ with eigenvalue $\gamma$, the <Rayleigh quotient> bounds give
$$
\gamma=\langle\alpha v,v\rangle+\langle\beta v,v\rangle\in[a+c,b+d].
$$
Thus **every eigenvalue of $\alpha+\beta$ lies in $[a+c,b+d]$**.

= 11G
